ld, we would drop n1 and n3, leaving only root and n5. But then retained_node_ids would be [\"root\",\"n5\"] which is not consistent across fixtures? In second, non-removable: root(1.0), n1(0.582>=), n3(0.0576<), n5(0.769>=) -> would keep root, n1, n5 (3 nodes). Third: non-removable: root(1.0), n1(0.0727<), n3(0.208<), n5(0.589>=) -> keep root, n5 (2 nodes). So varying sizes. But the output of solve is not provided, so we don't know expected. However, the problem statement says \"utility_threshold\" is present. Likely it is used. Perhaps we need to prune the tree: remove nodes that are removable and have utility below threshold, and also their descendants. But then for non-removable nodes, we always keep them regardless of utility? That seems plausible. The retained_node_ids would be: all non-removable nodes plus any removable nodes that have utility >= threshold? Let's test: In first fixture, removable: n2 (0.410<), n4 (0.177<), n6 (0.332<) all below, so only non-removable kept. In second: removable: n2 (0.444<), n4 (0.4777<), n6 (0.509<) all below, only non-removable kept. In third: removable: n2 (0.941>=), n4 (0.0953<), n6 (0.968>=). So if we keep removable nodes with utility>=threshold, then we would also keep n2 and n6. That would give retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6 (6 nodes). That would be a larger set. But the third fixture has n2 as removable and high utility, so likely it should be retained. That would give different results across fixtures. However, the problem may require that we treat removable nodes as not automatically removed; they are only removed if their utility is below threshold. And non-removable nodes are always kept. That is a common pruning strategy: prune subtrees where the node's utility is below threshold and the node is marked removable.\n\nThus we need to revisit. Let's examine the description: \"utility_threshold\" suggests a cutoff. The nodes have a \"removable\" flag. Possibly the task: given a tree, remove all nodes that are marked as removable and have utility < threshold, and also remove their descendants. Keep all other nodes. Then return the IDs of the retained nodes and their visit order (presumably DFS preorder).\n\nThat makes sense: nodes flagged removable are eligible for removal if their utility is below threshold; otherwise they stay. Non-removable nodes always stay.\n\nIn first fixture: removable nodes with utility<0.55: n2 (0.41), n4 (0.177), n6 (0.332) -> all removable and below, so removed. Keep all non-removable (root, n1, n3, n5) plus any removable above threshold (none). So retained: root, n1, n3, n5. Same as before.\n\nIn second: removable below: n2 (0.444), n4 (0.478? 0.4777 <0.55? Actually 0.4777 <0.55, yes), n6 (0.509 <0.55). So all removable below, retained same: root,n1,n3,n5.\n\nIn third: removable: n2 (0.941 >=0.55, so not removed), n4 (0.0953 <0.55, removed), n6 (0.968 >=0.55, not removed). So retained nodes: all non-removable (root, n1, n3, n5) plus removable n2 and n6. That's 6 nodes. Then visit_order would be DFS preorder, skipping the removed node n4 and its subtree (n4 has no children). But n2 and n6 are kept. Order: root, n1 (has child n6 kept), then n6 (child of n1), then back to root, then n2 (kept, and its child n4 is removed so skip), then n3 (kept, has child n5 kept), then n5. So order: root, n1, n6, n2, n3, n5.\n\nThis seems plausible. Note that in the third fixture, n2 is a removable node with high utility, so it is retained. That would differentiate outputs.\n\nWe don't have expected outputs, but we must infer the correct logic. Since the task is \"SE-PE-02\", likely it's about \"prune by utility threshold with removable flag\". The fixtures show varied utilities and removable flags. The presence of utility_threshold suggests it is used. So we'll implement: Build tree. DFS from root. When visiting a node, if the node is removable and its utility < threshold, then we skip that node and entire subtree (do not add to retained, do not recurse). Otherwise,