nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    # preserve order: as nodes appear, children list is built in order of encountering nodes as children. But children list for a parent should be in order of the nodes list? In fixtures, children appear in order: root children: n1, n2, n3 (as in nodes list). n1's child: n6, n2's child: n4, n3's child: n5. That's consistent. So using the iteration order of the nodes list will give children in that order because we append when we see the child node. That works.\n    order = []\n    def dfs(uid):\n        node = node_map[uid]\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\n            return\n        order.append(uid)\n        for child_id in children.get(uid, []):\n            dfs(child_id)\n    dfs(root_id)\n    return {\"retained_node_ids\": order, \"visit_order\": order}\n\nCheck: In case root is removable? It is not, but if it were, the condition would apply. But root likely never removable.\n\nNow we must output the implementation as JSON string.\n\nWe need to produce the JSON response with \"implementation\" key containing escaped Python source.\n\nSo final answer: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    order = []\\n    def dfs(uid):\\n        node = node_map[uid]\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\\n            return\\n        order.append(uid)\\n        for child_id in children.get(uid, []):\\n            dfs(child_id)\\n    dfs(root_id)\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': order, 'visit_order': order}\"}\n\nBut ensure it's properly escaped. Use double quotes, escape backslashes and newlines.\n\nLet's write the Python source as a string with escaped newlines.\n\nSource:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\n    order = []\n    def dfs(uid):\n        node = node_map[uid]\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] < threshold:\n            return\n        order.append(uid)\n        for child_id in children.get(uid, []):\n            dfs(child_id)\n    dfs(root_id)\n    return {'retained_node_ids': order, 'visit_order': order}\n\nNow convert to JSON string: replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". Also indent for readability? The JSON string value can be a single line, but we can keep the newlines escaped. That's fine.\n\nI'll produce the JSON object.\n\nMake sure no extra spaces or characters.\n\nFinal response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1864,"completion_tokens":5051,"total_tokens":6915,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4845},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":72},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}